<h1>Video 061 — static block</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: OOPs(Object Oriented Programming) Part-11 ||static block</p>
<p>ASR decode (Whisper): post-tumatum = post-mortem; keep subiting = keep writing; yet that time = at the time; register native = registerNatives; resulta set = ResultSet; Kourjava = core Java; immorietly / Immuriate = immediately; ATM = automatic; Yaman / Yam one = m1; Deraiyudha / deri would = Derived; Pasta = first; Swapyap / S.P.Yav = SOP (System.out.println); RIWO / read and directly write only = RIWO (Read Indirectly Write Only); shadam da JVM = shut down the JVM; No such method error column main = `NoSuchMethodError: main`; Kuchin = question; Validering = valid; Chaila / chile = child; base 1 / derived 1 = demo filenames Base1 / Derived1.</p>
<h3>00:04 — Agenda: post-mortem on static block</h3>
<p>Next topic after static control flow (Video 060): a post-mortem on the static block.</p>
<p>Points to cover:</p>
<ul><li>What is the need of a static block?</li></ul>
<ul><li>How do we declare it?</li></ul>
<ul><li>How many static blocks can we take?</li></ul>
<ul><li>When is a static block executed?</li></ul>
<h3>00:34 — When is a static block executed?</h3>
<p>Rule: A static block is executed at the time of class loading.</p>
<p>Therefore: at the time of class loading, if we want to perform any activity, we must define that activity inside a static block.</p>
<p>When should we go for a static block?</p>
<p>At the time of class loading, if we want to perform any activity → go for a static block.</p>
<pre class='code'>class DemoNeed {
    static {
        // activity that must run when the class is loaded
        System.out.println(&quot;class loading activity&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
    }
}
// prints:
 // class loading activity
 // main</pre>
<h3>02:34 — When is static block required? (motivation)</h3>
<p>Doubt: Sir, when exactly is a static block required?</p>
<p>Two classic real-world examples:</p>
<ol><li>Loading native libraries at class-load time</li></ol>
<ol><li>Registering a JDBC driver with DriverManager at class-load time</li></ol>
<h3>02:47 — Example 1: load native libraries at class loading</h3>
<p>At the time of Java class loading, the corresponding native libraries should be loaded. That activity belongs in a static block.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.loadLibrary(&quot;native_library_path&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>System.loadLibrary(...) takes the native library name/path. When class Test is loaded, the static block runs and the native library is loaded.</p>
<p>This is not only for our classes. Every predefined class that needs native libraries also does this at class-loading time.</p>
<h3>06:01 — Proof from JDK: `Object` and `Thread`</h3>
<h4>`Object.java` (idea from JDK source)</h4>
<p>java.lang.Object has several native methods (hashCode(), wait(), …). So the corresponding native library must be loaded when Object is loaded.</p>
<p>Inside Object, there is a static block that calls something like `registerNatives()` (same idea as System.loadLibrary):</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Object {
    private static native void registerNatives();

    static {
        registerNatives();
    }

    // native methods such as hashCode(), wait(), etc.
}</pre>
<h4>`Thread.java`</h4>
<p>Thread also has native methods (start(), yield(), …). Same pattern:</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Thread implements Runnable {
    private static native void registerNatives();

    static {
        registerNatives();
    }

    // native start(), yield(), etc.
}</pre>
<p>Takeaway: If an activity must happen exactly when the class is loaded, put it in a static block. Loading native libraries is one such area.</p>
<h3>07:53 — Example 2: JDBC driver registration (very important)</h3>
<p>Typical basic JDBC steps (core Java):</p>
<ol><li>Load driver class → Class.forName(&quot;driver.class.Name&quot;)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Get connection → DriverManager.getConnection(...)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Prepare statement</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute query</li></ol>
<ol><li>Use ResultSet to read data</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// conceptual JDBC flow (not a full runnable app)
Class.forName(&quot;oracle.jdbc.driver.OracleDriver&quot;); // load driver class
// Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pwd);
// PreparedStatement pst = con.prepareStatement(sql);
// ResultSet rs = pst.executeQuery();</pre>
<h4>The puzzle</h4>
<p>A machine may have multiple DB drivers installed (Oracle, MySQL, DB2, …).</p>
<p>After Class.forName(...), we immediately ask DriverManager for a connection. We never explicitly “register” the driver with DriverManager in our application code.</p>
<p>Question: How does DriverManager know about the driver class we just loaded?</p>
<h4>The answer: static block inside every driver class</h4>
<p>As per the JDBC specification, every database driver class must contain a static block that registers this driver with `DriverManager`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class DBDriver {
    static {
        // register this driver with DriverManager
        // (conceptual — real drivers call DriverManager.registerDriver(...))
    }
}
// what happens when we write:
Class.forName(&quot;com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver&quot;);
// 1) Driver class is loaded
// 2) its static block runs automatically
// 3) driver registers itself with DriverManager
// → we are NOT responsible to register explicitly</pre>
<p>If we design our own driver class, this static-block registration is compulsory (JDBC spec). That is why the “register with DriverManager” step is not listed as a separate app step after Class.forName.</p>
<p>Need of static block (summary):</p>
<p>At the time of class loading, if we want to perform certain activity automatically, go for a static block.</p>
<h3>15:04 — How many static blocks in a class?</h3>
<p>Within a class we can declare any number of static blocks.</p>
<p>All of them execute from top to bottom.</p>
<pre class='code'>class MultiStatic {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;first static block&quot;);
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;second static block&quot;);
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;third static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
    }
}
// prints:
 // first static block
 // second static block
 // third static block
 // main</pre>
<h3>16:23 — Interview Q1: print without `main`?</h3>
<p>Question 1: Without writing a main method, is it possible to print some statements to the console?</p>
<p>Answer: Yes — by using a static block (valid through Java 1.6; see version note later).</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>What happens (≤ 1.6):</p>
<ol><li>Class loads → static block runs → prints Hello I can print</li></ol>
<ol><li>JVM then looks for main</li></ol>
<ol><li>main is missing → `NoSuchMethodError: main`</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// runtime behavior without System.exit(0):
// prints Hello I can print
// then: Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: main</pre>
<p>To stop JVM from searching for main, call `System.exit(0)` inside the static block:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hello I can print
// (JVM exits; no NoSuchMethodError)</pre>
<p>Demo without exit (compile OK, then error after print):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
    }
}
// prints Hello I can print
// then NoSuchMethodError: main</pre>
<p>Demo with exit:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hello I can print</pre>
<h3>21:10 — Interview Q2: without `main` **and** without static block?</h3>
<p>Question 2: Without writing main and without writing a static block, is it possible to print to the console?</p>
<p>First instinct: No.</p>
<p>Correct answer (≤ 1.6): Yes — there are multiple ways.</p>
<p>Constraint reminder:</p>
<ul><li>Do not write main</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not write a static block</li></ul>
<ul><li>Still print something</li></ul>
<h3>22:41 — Way 1: static variable assignment → static method</h3>
<p>Declare a static variable whose initialization calls a static method. That method prints and then exits.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static int x = m1();

    public static int m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
        return 10; // syntactically required (return type int)
    }
}</pre>
<p>Static control flow (idea):</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members (x, m1)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of static variable assignments → x = m1() runs → print → System.exit(0)</li></ol>
<p>No main, no static block — still prints.</p>
<p>Variant shown in lab (message changed):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static int x = m1();

    public static int m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hi I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
        return 10;
    }
}
// prints: Hi I can print</pre>
<h3>25:37 — Way 2: static object + instance block</h3>
<p>Create an object while initializing a static reference. Object creation runs the instance block.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static Test t = new Test();

    {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hello I can print</pre>
<p>Why it works:</p>
<ol><li>Identify static members (t)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute static assignment t = new Test() → object creation → instance block runs</li></ol>
<p>Instance block runs at object creation time (unlike static block, which runs at class loading).</p>
<p>Demo with different text:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static Test t = new Test();

    {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hi I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hi I can print</pre>
<h3>27:36 — Way 3: static object + constructor</h3>
<p>Same idea: static reference = new Test(), but print from the constructor.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static Test t = new Test();

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hello I can print</pre>
<p>Lab variant:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static Test t = new Test();

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello Hi I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// prints: Hello Hi I can print</pre>
<p>Q2 summary: Without main and without static block — yes, multiple ways (static init calling a method; instance block; constructor), up to Java 1.6.</p>
<p>Board recap of way 1:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static int x = m1();

    public static int m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
        return 10;
    }
}</pre>
<h3>32:43 — Version twist (very important)</h3>
<p>Everything above (print without main / without static block) applies only up to Java 1.6.</p>
<p>From Java 1.7 onwards:</p>
<ul><li>`main` method is mandatory to start program execution</li></ul>
<ul><li>Without writing main, it is impossible to print statements to the console (for a normal java ClassName launch)</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Java ≥ 1.7: this alone is NOT enough to start a program
class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello I can print&quot;);
        System.exit(0);
    }
}
// From 1.7 onwards: JVM requires main to start execution
 // (behavior / error messaging differs by version; exam point: main is mandatory)</pre>
<p>Note (board):</p>
<p>From 1.7 V onwards, main method is mandatory to start a program execution.</p>
<p>Hence, without writing main, it is impossible to print some statements to the console.</p>
<p>(Same point was also covered in the main method fundamentals lectures.)</p>
<h3>35:09 — Next topic: static control flow in parent–child</h3>
<p>Up to here: need of static block, native/JDBC examples, multiple static blocks, interview tricks + 1.7 rule.</p>
<p>Next important terminology: static control flow in parent-to-child relationship — if parent and child both have static members, what is the control flow when we run the child?</p>
<h3>36:09 — Example: `Base` and `Derived`</h3>
<p>Two classes, one compilation unit (e.g. Base.java containing both). Derived is the same shape as the single-class static-control-flow example from Video 060 (two static blocks, main, helper method).</p>
<h4>Parent: `Base`</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Base {
    static int i = 10;

    static {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base main&quot;);
    }

    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(j);
    }

    static int j = 20;
}</pre>
<p>(Compared with the previous single-class demo: second static block in parent is omitted here; otherwise same idea.)</p>
<h4>Child: `Derived`</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Derived extends Base {
    static int x = 100;

    static {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived first static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived main&quot;);
    }

    public static void m2() {
        System.out.println(y);
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived second static block&quot;);
    }

    static int y = 200;
}</pre>
<p>Combined file sketch:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Base {
    static int i = 10;
    static {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base static block&quot;);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base main&quot;);
    }
    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(j);
    }
    static int j = 20;
}

class Derived extends Base {
    static int x = 100;
    static {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived first static block&quot;);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived main&quot;);
    }
    public static void m2() {
        System.out.println(y);
    }
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived second static block&quot;);
    }
    static int y = 200;
}</pre>
<h3>41:03 — Compile and run the child</h3>
<p>javac Base.java</p>
<p>Generates two .class files:</p>
<ul><li>Base.class</li></ul>
<ul><li>Derived.class</li></ul>
<p>Run the child:</p>
<p>java Derived</p>
<p>Key idea: Parent class members are available to the child by default. So whenever we load the child class, the parent class is also loaded, and parent static control flow runs first.</p>
<h3>42:44 — Three activities when executing the child</h3>
<p>Whenever we execute the child class, static control flow does these three activities:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members from parent to child (top to bottom: parent first, then child)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of static variable assignments and static blocks from parent to child</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of only the child class `main` method (we launched java Derived, not java Base)</li></ol>
<p>When executing child class:
  1) Identify static members: parent → child
  2) Execute static assignments &amp; static blocks: parent → child
  3) Execute only child class main method</p>
<p>If the child has no main, then the parent main would run (inherited). Here child already has main, so parent main is not executed.</p>
<h3>45:24 — Trace step 1: identification (RIWO)</h3>
<p>During identification, static variables get default values and enter RIWO state (Read Indirectly / Write Only — same idea as Video 060).</p>
<p>Parent (`Base`) — identify in order:</p>
<p>Child (`Derived`) — continue numbering:</p>
<p>Steps 1–11 = identification complete.</p>
<h3>47:12 — Trace step 2: assignments &amp; static blocks (parent → child)</h3>
<p>Parent execution (continues numbering):</p>
<pre class='code'>// during Base static block, j is still 0
public static void m1() {
    System.out.println(j); // prints 0
}</pre>
<p>Child execution:</p>
<pre class='code'>// during Derived first static block, y is still 0
public static void m2() {
    System.out.println(y); // prints 0
}</pre>
<p>Steps 12–22 = second activity complete.</p>
<h3>50:56 — Trace step 3: only child `main`</h3>
<p>Steps 23–25.</p>
<h3>52:02 — Expected output: `java Derived`</h3>
<p>0
Base static block
0
Derived first static block
Derived second static block
200
Derived main</p>
<p>Full runnable program (same as board):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Base {
    static int i = 10;

    static {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m1();
        System.out.println(&quot;Base main&quot;);
    }

    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(j);
    }

    static int j = 20;
}

class Derived extends Base {
    static int x = 100;

    static {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived first static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m2();
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived main&quot;);
    }

    public static void m2() {
        System.out.println(y);
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Derived second static block&quot;);
    }

    static int y = 200;
}</pre>
<p>javac Base.java
java Derived
// prints:
 // 0
 // Base static block
 // 0
 // Derived first static block
 // Derived second static block
 // 200
 // Derived main</p>
<p>Lab used names like Base1 / Derived1 — same program, same output when running the child.</p>
<h3>53:56 — Theory board (parent → child)</h3>
<p>Whenever we are executing the child class, the following sequence runs automatically as per static control flow:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members from parent to child (steps ~1–11)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of static variable assignments and static blocks from parent to child (steps ~12–22)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of only child class main method (steps ~23–25)</li></ol>
<p>Color hint from lecture (for notebook):</p>
<ul><li>Red boxes → identification</li></ul>
<ul><li>Green boxes → assignments / static blocks</li></ul>
<ul><li>Blue boxes → child main</li></ul>
<h3>1:02:41 — Child `main` vs parent `main`</h3>
<p>We are executing Derived, so Derived.main runs.</p>
<ul><li>If Derived does not contain main, then Base.main would execute (available via inheritance).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Here Derived does contain main, so Base.main is not executed.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If we execute the parent (java Base), parent main runs (child not involved).</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// if Derived had NO main:
class DerivedNoMain extends Base {
    static int x = 100;
    // ... static blocks / m2 / y ...
    // no main → Base.main would run after parent+child static init
}</pre>
<h3>1:05:44 — Run the parent instead: `java Base`</h3>
<p>Same program, but:</p>
<p>java Base</p>
<p>Question: When we execute the child, parent is loaded. When we execute the parent, is the child loaded?</p>
<p>No. Parent members are available to the child, but child members are not available to the parent. So loading/executing Base does not load Derived.</p>
<p>Only Base static control flow:</p>
<ol><li>Identify Base static members</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute Base static assignments &amp; static block → m1() prints `j == 0`, then &quot;Base static block&quot;, then j = 20</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute Base main → m1() prints `20`, then &quot;Base main&quot;</li></ol>
<p>java Base
// prints:
 // 0
 // Base static block
 // 20
 // Base main</p>
<p>Side-by-side:</p>
<pre class='code'>// java Derived  →  0, Base static block, 0, Derived first..., Derived second..., 200, Derived main
// java Base     →  0, Base static block, 20, Base main</pre>
<h3>1:08:14 — Closing rules (must remember)</h3>
<ol><li>Whenever we are loading the CHILD class → parent class will be loaded automatically (parent members must be available to child).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Whenever we are loading the PARENT class → child class will NOT be loaded (child members are not available to parent by default).</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// conceptual
// load Derived  ⇒  Base loaded first, then Derived
// load Base     ⇒  only Base  (Derived untouched)</pre>
<p>Loading CHILD  → parent loaded automatically
Loading PARENT → child will NOT be loaded
  (because child members by default won&#x27;t be available to the parent)</p>
<p>These are important loopholes / exam traps for static control flow with inheritance.</p>
<h3>1:10:45 — End of video</h3>
<p>Covered in this Part-11 lecture:</p>
<ul><li>Need &amp; timing of static block (class loading)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Native library loading + JDK registerNatives pattern</li></ul>
<ul><li>JDBC driver auto-registration via static block</li></ul>
<ul><li>Any number of static blocks, top-to-bottom</li></ul>
<ul><li>Print without main (static block / other tricks) ≤ 1.6; `main` mandatory from 1.7</li></ul>
<ul><li>Static control flow: parent → child (3 steps, full trace, java Derived vs java Base)</li></ul>
<p>Next videos continue instance control flow / mixing instance + static (see playlist Parts after this).</p>
